The guide explains how to use the map and what the data contains. It cannot:
- give legal, planning, tax or cadastral advice, or say what can be built;
- give information that the map does not contain, such as vehicle delays;
- answer about Comune services not on the map (taxes, registry office, applications);
- confirm that a single data item is correct: every profile gives its source, to be checked with the rights holder.

In these cases you are invited to consult the original source or the competent offices. To report an error in the data, something that does not work or an idea to improve the site, open an issue on GitHub (a free account is enough): the «Open an issue on GitHub» button is in the «Contribute and report issues» section of the introduction page.

When you click on a monument, the profile has the name as its title and the **category** as its badge. Below there are, when available:
- the **photo**;
- the **description**;
- the link **«Go to the Municipality website»** (Tourism Portal of the Municipality of Palermo);
- the source: «Tourism Portal — Municipality of Palermo», or the source given for that place.

If the description says that the building no longer exists (for example «non più esistente» (no longer existing), «demolito» (demolished), «abbattuto» (pulled down) or «distrutto» (destroyed)), the profile shows a **notice** of a lost building. If the description is only that phrase, it is not repeated as text.

The popup on the map shows title, category, photo, description, link and source. Where the monument has been matched to a building, the building is also coloured on the map; the buildings of lost monuments are not coloured.

At the top of the profile there are:
- the **title** (that of the main section of the point; if there is none, «Place profile»);
- the **coordinates** of the point clicked, in degrees with five decimals, for example «38.11500° N, 13.36100° E»;
- the territorial **context**: «District [name]», «Neighbourhood [name]» and «UPL [name]» (only those available);
- the **tabs**, with arrows to scroll them right and left. Home and End go to the first and the last, the keyboard arrows move from one to the next. If a tab has more than one section, a number next to its name says how many there are.

On a smartphone the profile rises from the bottom like a sheet; a handle changes the height of the profile.

The link contains the **visible state** of the app:
- the **position, zoom, rotation and tilt** of the map;
- the **layers that are on** and their **opacity** (if lower than the maximum);
- the chosen **basemap**;
- the **filters** by district, neighbourhood or UPL, by transport line and by year and severity of accidents;
- the **light or dark theme**;
- some preferences saved in the browser: the colours chosen for the layers, the layer order and the drawing order, and the layers added from the RNDT catalogue, «My layers» and services (see below).

Whoever opens the link sees that state applied again as if they had made the clicks themselves. If the link names layers or backgrounds that no longer exist in the app, the app ignores them.

**What does not travel in the link.** Of the layers added by the user, only what the recipient can reopen from a **public address** goes: files uploaded from the computer do not go, nor do services with username and password or with a token, nor addresses with parameters that look secret (token, key, password, signature). The window says so: «[n] layers you added (files or services with credentials) are not in the link.»

**Link too long.** The link has a maximum length: if the view is very rich, custom colours and added layers may be removed and the window warns «The view is very rich: custom colours or added layers are not in the link.» The state is compressed (format `v1z.`, with a fallback to JSON if the browser cannot compress). A link that is too long or not valid is ignored when opened.

The button with the circular arrow next to the search has two effects:
- if there is a **selection** on the map (areas highlighted by the click), it removes it, like Esc;
- with the map already clear it asks for confirmation: «Reset colours, panels, layer order and the click prompt to their initial values? Added RNDT layers are kept.» On confirming it deletes from the browser the **colours of the layers**, the **information chosen for the profiles**, the **layer order** and the choice not to show the initial prompt, then reloads the page. The added RNDT catalogue layers are not touched.

**Consent banner.** The site uses **Google Analytics** (with cookies) to count visits in aggregate form, **only if you accept**: nothing is loaded beforehand. The banner says «We use Google Analytics, with cookies, to count visits in aggregate form. It only starts if you consent; you can change your mind at any time.» with **Privacy notice**, **Decline** and **Accept**. The choice is saved in the browser; if you decline, the `_ga` cookies are deleted. On the Privacy page the **«Manage consent to statistics»** button reopens the choice.

**The Privacy page** (a concise notice under EU Regulation 2016/679 and the ePrivacy directive) says that:
- the map **requires no registration** and does not collect personal data through forms; the data shown (cadastre, population, offices…) are public;
- for statistics Google Analytics (Google Ireland Ltd., EU) is used only with consent; Google may set cookies and process the IP address and device information, with possible transfers outside the EU under the Data Privacy Framework;
- **what stays on the device** is only the theme choice, the app's offline cache (service worker) and, for those who use them, the layers loaded from one's own computer or from a service indicated (IndexedDB): nothing is sent to the controllers;
- **user and password** of protected services are kept only in memory, for the session;
- **external services contacted by the browser**: the OpenFreeMap basemap, the videos on YouTube in «enhanced privacy» mode (only if you press Play) and a proxy on Cloudflare Workers to query the RNDT catalogues; each sees the IP address of whoever connects;
- **rights** (arts. 15–22 GDPR): access, rectification, erasure, restriction, objection; complaints to the Garante per la protezione dei dati personali (the Italian data protection authority);
- **data controller**: OpenDataSicilia, reachable from the GitHub discussions or from the site opendatasicilia.it.

Other external services mentioned elsewhere: the Street View panorama is Google's (see `41-street-view.md`); some basemaps are served by Google, Esri and OpenStreetMap (see `47-mappe-di-base-e-storiche.md`).

The app has a **service worker** that makes repeat visits immediate and keeps the **interface available even without a network**:
- the **page** and the **app's data** (data folder: charging points every hour, fuel stations every day, fires, PAI…) are requested from the network first, with the saved copy as a fallback;
- the **static files** (scripts, styles, images, fonts) are served immediately from the copy and updated in the background;
- the **PMTiles files** read in ranges are kept for 24 hours, so they are not downloaded again at every visit;
- everything else (other sites, requests that send data) is left untouched.

So offline you see what has already been opened; the external services (basemaps, orthophotos, Street View, catalogues) need the network.

The site has a **manifest** («Palermo Digital Twin», short name «Palermo DT», standalone full screen, with the icons) that allows it to be installed as an app from the browser.

Each layer of the group is a row with:
- the **checkbox with the eye** to switch the layer on and off on the map (it is the same checkbox that drives the Topics tab);
- the **«Opacity»** slider (from 0 to 1, in steps of 0.05) under the row, to make it more or less transparent; the chosen value stays even if the group is redrawn;
- the bin **«Remove layer [name]»**, which removes the layer from the map and from the list;
- the **arrows** and **dragging** to change the stacking order, as in the other groups (the order is saved in the browser). In addition, the **«Layer order on the map»** card in the Layers tab lists all the layers that are on, including those of RNDT and of «My layers» (see `45-barra-strati-legende-e-ordine.md`).

The layers of the group are always listed in **alphabetical order** by name; the arrows and dragging affect the order on the map, not the list.

A note in brackets can appear next to the name:
- **«(this session only)»**: the layer could not be saved in the browser and stays while the page is open;
- **«(loading errors)»**: the service gave errors during loading;
- **«(unavailable)»**: the layer is not available; the checkbox is disabled.

The layers are saved by themselves in the browser and come back when it is reopened, on or off as they were. A layer whose address is known is saved with the address; data with no known address are saved in the browser up to 5 MB per layer. The «Reset» button of the filters asks for confirmation and says that «Added RNDT layers are kept».
